oding in areas that\nhave recently received excessive moisture, or have recently\nexperienced flooding.\n- The greatest risk of flooding will be along the Conejos River and\nthe South Fork of the Rio Grande, including South Fork and\nMogote.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T12:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Upper Rio Grande Valley/Eastern San Juan Mountains Below 10000 Ft; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.a86d832769b4b479ba2fd2815cb3755bcf6b1d9a.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.a86d832769b4b479ba2fd2815cb3755bcf6b1d9a.001.1"},{"event":"Winter Storm Warning","headline":"Winter Storm Warning issued October 31 at 11:03AM MDT until November 2 at 11:00AM MST by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Heavy snow expected. Total snow accumulations 8 to 14\ninches. Winds gusting as high as 45 mph.\n\n* WHERE...Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Feet.\n\n* WHEN...From 5 PM Sunday to 11 AM MST Monday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Travel could be very difficult, especially Sunday night\nand Monday morning. Strong winds could produce areas of blowing and\ndrifting snow.","ends":"2026-11-02T12:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.972dce7f6822bb9f6a918d407616194a7550c94d.001.2","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.972dce7f6822bb9f6a918d407616194a7550c94d.001.2"},{"event":"Winter Storm Warning","headline":"Winter Storm Warning issued October 31 at 10:37AM MDT until November 2 at 12:00PM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Heavy snow expected above 10, 000 feet. Total snow\naccumulations between 12 and 18 inches, with locally higher amounts\npossible. Winds gusting as high as 45 mph.\n\n* WHERE...Northwest San Juan Mountains and Southwest San Juan\nMountains.\n\n* WHEN...From 6 AM Sunday to noon MST Monday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Travel could be very difficult to impossible. The\nhazardous conditions will impact the Monday morning commute.","ends":"2026-11-02T12:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.2bdd41b860e09ab541155db973dc91e9b1c8543e.001.2","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.2bdd41b860e09ab541155db973dc91e9b1c8543e.001.2"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 30 at 3:02PM MDT until November 2 at 11:00AM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of southwest Colorado, including the following\nareas, Animas River Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, San\nJuan River Basin and Southwest San Juan Mountains and southeast\nUtah, including the following areas, Canyonlands/Natural Bridges,\nLa Sal and Abajo Mountains and Southeast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...From late Saturday night through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, recently burned areas, and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations. Low-water crossings may be flooded. Storm\ndrains and ditches may become clogged with debris.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- 1 to 3 inches of rain is expected across portions of southeast\nUtah and southwest Colorado from Sunday morning through Monday\nmorning over soils that are still saturated from recent rainfall\nand flooding.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T11:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Southwest San Juan Mountains; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.d9e2fd526e1e61f9532cd34833bc42a40d097b54.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.d9e2fd526e1e61f9532cd34833bc42a40d097b54.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-31T23: